417] [17592186521437 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521437 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521437 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520962 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521437 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "dad03ac8-e6b0-55d6-a367-f056cb5c48e0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521450 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521450 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521450 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521450 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520817 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521450 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f6517593-c488-552c-8243-888093248acc" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1069 "q-24805" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/19/197557b866b4a4a1c2c151c29cc529e12a5048b941223fe8e2eb4a7c4959223e.png)\n\nThe diagonals of a square increase in length at a rate of $1.5\\text{ mm/min}.$ At what rate is the area of the square changing when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "08e98843-e2bc-5a10-8ff2-9ed917780859" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521454 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $1.5\\,\\text{mm/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.5.\n$$\n\nAlso, when the diagonals are $4\\,\\text{mm},$ we have $h=4.$ Substituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=1.5\\cdot 4=6{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $6\\,{\\text{mm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521389 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116055" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/eb/eb40860015e5404f3680b22835149ddc63baf80cd8e1f34e30f936308c3221e0.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square decrease at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min}$, at what rate is the perimeter decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f159c95-e1be-591f-b6cb-f9ebf9404573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521457 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is decreasing at a rate of $10\\sqrt{2}\\text{ mm}\\text{/min},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=-10\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(-10\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=-40\\text{ mm/min}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square decreases at $40\\text{ mm}/\\text{min}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521355 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314510" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/00/007ac521cd9bd97773aa18e243e788ce9eb473a65e3242022539266bc8f23ef4.png)\n\nA laser technician is reducing the diagonals of a square aperture at a rate of $2.4\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the aperture decreasing when the diagonals are $12.5\\,\\text{mm}$ long each?\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe area is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "37d3dfcf-127d-51c3-8809-c6a386eec680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521480 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2.4\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=12.5.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.4\\cdot 12.5=-30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square aperture is decreasing at a rate of $30{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521437 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1069 "q-116049" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/bb/bbd79b56420475595834e8cd67d4b9d3d5ca8a2e0fda399f49de1f4e9bb26c95.png)\n\nIf the diagonals of a square increase at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour}$, at what rate is the perimeter changing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5082ae1f-b15c-594a-83e6-841946d45040" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521497 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $p$ be the perimeter of the square, and $h$ be the length of the diagonal. The relationship between $p$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\np=2\\sqrt{2}h.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(p) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(2\\sqrt{2}h) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each diagonal of the square is increasing at a rate of $4\\sqrt{2}\\text{ feet}\\text{/hour},$ so we have \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=4\\sqrt{2}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information into the above expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t}$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}p}{\\text{d}t} & =(4\\sqrt{2})\\cdot (2\\sqrt{2})=16\\text{ feet/hour}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the perimeter of the square increases at $16\\text{ feet}/\\text{hour}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521450 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344456" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/min}.$ Given that $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square, respectively, find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ where $t$ is the time in minutes, as a function of $x.$\n\n$\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=$\n{{field-1}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5e8039e3-82ac-52bc-a02f-bab2dbe2062b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521503 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate, and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/e2/e2b8bf4ff974ba499167a4349c894ea3c4c87cc14c731cf806e47d58c8c6e027.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=24\\text{ }{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{min}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521508