1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521524 #edb/ref 1071 "The sides (in meters) of a square are decreasing. At a particular moment, the rate of decrease of the area of the square is numerically equal to ten times the rate of decrease of the sides. What is the length of the sides of the square at that moment?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521524 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cec0878-9352-5be3-a087-f066a0b92d19" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521524 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe given information tells us that $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ So, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}$ in the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2x\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ 10\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} & =2x\\cdot \\cancel{\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}} \\\\ 10 & =2x \\\\ x & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, at that particular moment we have $x=5\\text{ m}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/34/34a41e39a36d3e073ef2ca4144bd346b340efa22a9da7d3e8a9df48d32d900b3.png)\n\nA square opening in a testing chamber is being reduced, causing the area of the opening to decrease at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate are the diagonals decreasing when each diagonal measures $35\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\n*Hint*: Assume that the rate of decrease is positive.\n\nThe diagonal is decreasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} $\\text{mm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9793c42f-eccf-5af0-a22e-1cfcdfee54a8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521530 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $A$ be the area of the square opening, and $h$ be the length of its diagonal. The relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating the relationship between $A$ and $h$ with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{h}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $49{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}=-49.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the diagonals when the diagonal has length $h=35.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{35}\\cdot (-49) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-\\frac{49}{35} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-1.4\\text{ mm/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the diagonal of the opening is decreasing at a rate of \n$1.4\\text{ mm/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1071 "The area of a square increases at a rate of $24{\\text{ m}}^{2}\\text{/hour}.$ If $x$ and $A$ denote the side length and area of the square respectively, then find an expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}$, where $t$ is the time in hours." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "9a2565d5-0f88-53cc-81b1-7e0ad2dcfafe" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521533 #edb/ref 1074 "Here, the area is changing at a constant rate,  and we want to know the rate at which the side lengths are changing.\n\nFirst, let's draw a diagram:\n\n![](images/d7/d713fe1a9286ec36d413bda32cce5ea4327003e6cc4f8a40a45cb945166d68c1.png)\n\nWe want to find $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.$ The area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe know from the given information that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =24\\text{ m}{}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting this information, we reach\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}24 & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{12}{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521431 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1069 "q-314491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/e8/e8944ed32d3019a0d0d1ccebbc2f40c5248c221f3cddba237906db509fac0737.png)\n\nThe sides of a square are increasing in length at a rate of $8\\text{ ft/s}.$ At what rate is the area of ​​the square increasing when the side is $4\\,\\text{ft}$ long?\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}} ${\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b66e27f7-fcba-5767-8cef-431ef74750b6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521542 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $8\\,\\text{ft/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=8.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{ft},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =8\\cdot 2(4) \\\\  & =64{\\text{ ft}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $64\\,{\\text{ft}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1069 "q-73369" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/8b/8b4196d82bf27236e6c28651a01678c60c41bc851afbb7aad2e754578ba330e9.png)\n\nJacob is a carpenter. He wishes to reduce the size of a wooden square while maintaining its shape. The lengths of the diagonals of the square decrease at a rate of $2\\text{ cm}\\text{/h}.$ At what rate, in squared centimeters per hour, is the area of ​​the square decreasing when the length of the diagonal is $45\\,\\text{cm}?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c95a9e41-829a-5344-af50-15a5606363d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521546 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ decreases at a rate of $2\\,\\text{cm/h},$ so\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=45.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =-2\\cdot 45=-90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square is decreasing at a rate of $90\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}\\text{/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521375 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344649" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/cb/cb048e7ac152ffbc740d611726871f395b4c6c4abcc324a4eed890e6116ec814.png)\n\nA manufacturing process gradually increases the size of a square opening by lengthening its diagonals at a rate of $1.6\\text{ mm}\\text{/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the opening increasing when each diagonal measures $25\\,\\text{mm}?$\n\nThe area is increasing at a rate of\n{{field-1}}\n${\\text{mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "dd3d0969-d794-5861-a620-0bc6a8d07956" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521558 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $h$ and $A$ be the length of the diagonals and the area of the square, respectively. Using the Pythagorean theorem, it can be shown that the relationship between $A$ and $h$ is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nWe want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}h}(\\frac{{h}^{2}}{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot h.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that $h$ increases at a rate of $1.6\\,\\text{mm/s},$ so\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=1.6.\n$$\n\nAlso, we want to compute the rate of change of the area when the diagonal has length $h=25.$ Substituting this information into our expression for $\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t},$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =1.6\\cdot 25=40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe conclude that the area of the square opening is increasing at a rate of \n$40{\\text{ mm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521342 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1069 "q-2773" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1071 "![](images/fd/fd63afc83917b365d1b91bf4b033be10b64c76a18e7aa16cb8019dc8ef197eb1.png)\n\nThe sides of a square increase in length at a rate of $6\\text{ cm/s}.$ At what rate is the area of the square increasing when the sides are $4\\text{ cm}$ long?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e4eddc77-b22f-562d-ab08-be01c533b457" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521561 #edb/ref 1074 "Let $x$ and $A$ be the length of the sides and the area of the square, respectively. We want to find\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t}.\n$$\n\nThe area $A$ is related to side length $x$ by the formula\n\n$$\nA={x}^{2}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating this relationship with respect to $t$ using implicit differentiation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(A) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}({x}^{2}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}\\cdot 2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're told that each side of the square is increasing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{cm/s},$ so we have $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=6.$ Also, when the sides are $4\\,\\text{cm},$ we have $x=4.$ Substituting this information into the above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}A}{\\text{d}t} & =(6)\\cdot 2(4)=48{\\text{ cm}}^{2}\\text{/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of the square increases at $48\\,{\\text{cm}}^{2}/\\text{s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
